WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.150
<v Stephane>Next we have WAF, the Web Application Firewall,</v>

2
00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:04.920
which allows you to protect your web applications

3
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:07.740
from common web exploits at the layer seven.

4
00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:09.570
So this is the HTTP layer.

5
00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:11.850
You can deploy it on the application load balancer

6
00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:13.350
to get localized rules.

7
00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:15.720
You can deploy it on the API Gateway

8
00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:19.290
to get rules running at the regional or edge level.

9
00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:20.550
You can deploy it on CloudFront.

10
00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:21.930
And these rules are going to be

11
00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:24.480
running globally on all edge locations

12
00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:26.940
where CloudFront is, and this could be used for example,

13
00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:29.340
if you wanted to have a web application firewall

14
00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:32.610
for your classic load balancer, your EC2 instances,

15
00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:34.620
custom origins, or S3 websites,

16
00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:38.010
you would have CloudFront then deploy WAF on CloudFront.

17
00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:39.900
You can also deploy it on AppSync,

18
00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:41.220
and this is going to allow you

19
00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:44.190
to protect your GraphQL APIs directly.

20
00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:47.160
So remember that WAF is not used for DDoS protection.

21
00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:48.360
For this we have SHIELD.

22
00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:52.140
WAF is used for layer seven type of exploits.

23
00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:54.450
So for this you're going to define web ACL.

24
00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:56.700
So the Web Access Control List,

25
00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.850
and the rules can include IP addresses filtering,

26
00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:02.340
HTTP headers, HTTP body, URL strings.

27
00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:04.200
You can have common attacks protections

28
00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:08.640
such as SQL injection, or cross-site scripting, XXS.

29
00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:10.537
You can have size constraints to say,

30
00:01:10.537 --> 00:01:13.920
"Hey, any request bigger than 20 megabytes I don't want."

31
00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:15.300
You can have geomatch to block

32
00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:17.220
or allow a specific country,

33
00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:18.780
and you can have rate-based rules.

34
00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:20.737
So to count occurrences of events and say,

35
00:01:20.737 --> 00:01:23.100
"Hey, if this happened more than five seconds,

36
00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:26.550
five times per second, this looks fishy, block it."

37
00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:28.590
And the rule actions can be,

38
00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:29.580
allow or block,

39
00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:32.010
as we know to allow or to block traffic.

40
00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:33.960
They could be count, and that's an interesting one.

41
00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:37.320
It is to see if a rule would be in effect or not.

42
00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:40.110
So this allows you to detect false positives ahead of time.

43
00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:41.850
So you would just counter occurrences,

44
00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:44.550
and analyze the result of a count operation,

45
00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:46.650
but the traffic is still allowed through.

46
00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:49.230
So you do counts before Block, or CAPTCHA,

47
00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:50.880
or silent challenges to make sure

48
00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:52.980
that the traffic is not coming from bots

49
00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:54.600
but actually from humans.

50
00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:57.030
So something super important for you to know is

51
00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:58.620
that WAF has managed rules,

52
00:01:58.620 --> 00:01:59.640
and you need to know it for the exam.

53
00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:02.460
So there are over 190 managed rules,

54
00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:04.470
and I'll give you some of the most important.

55
00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:07.560
They are ready to use manage rules that will be from AWS,

56
00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:09.120
or the marketplace sellers,

57
00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:12.390
and they'll allow you to just power up WAF very quickly.

58
00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:13.890
So you get baseline rule groups,

59
00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:16.410
which is general protection from common threats,

60
00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:19.500
and this include the managed rule, common rule set,

61
00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:21.900
the managed rules admin protection rule set and so on.

62
00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:24.510
So they're just baseline for common protection.

63
00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:27.150
Then you have use-case specific rule groups.

64
00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:30.090
And they give you protection for many use cases,

65
00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:33.960
such as the SQL rule sets, the Windows rule sets.

66
00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:36.030
The PHP rule sets, the WordPress rule sets,

67
00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:39.000
to protect this kind of application.

68
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.550
Then you get IP reputation rule groups,

69
00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:45.120
and this is to block request based on the source IP.

70
00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.460
So for example, you have a malicious IP,

71
00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:49.260
and there is this very important rule called

72
00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:52.440
the Managed Rules Amazon IP Reputation List,

73
00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:55.470
which is a list of all the IPs that are trusted by AWS,

74
00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:57.300
and those that are not trusted by AWS.

75
00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:00.420
And this can help you block spams very quickly.

76
00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:04.500
You can also get anonymous IP list as a rule, and so on.

77
00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:07.950
And finally there's block bot control manage rule group

78
00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:10.110
to block and manage requests from bots.

79
00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:11.220
So this includes, for example,

80
00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:14.370
the AWS manage rule bots control rule sets.

81
00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:15.203
So as you can see,

82
00:03:15.203 --> 00:03:18.180
we have four kinds of managed rule buckets,

83
00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:21.360
baseline use case specific IP reputation and above control.

84
00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:24.000
And the thing that can come up in exam is one of these,

85
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.400
including the Amazon IP Reputation List.

86
00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:28.530
Okay, so what about logging for WAF?

87
00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:30.660
This is also something that can come up in the exam.

88
00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:34.170
So while WAF can send logs to Amazon CloudWatch log groups,

89
00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:37.980
and you get a debit of five megabytes per second maximum,

90
00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:39.960
you can also send it to Amazon S3 bucket,

91
00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:42.990
and the logs will be sent every five minutes.

92
00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:44.790
Or if you want to have more control,

93
00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:46.520
well you can send it directly from WAF

94
00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:47.910
to Kinesis Data Firehose,

95
00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:51.630
in which case you are only limited by the Firehose quotas.

96
00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:52.560
Well, why would you do this?

97
00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:54.780
Well, for example, if you have very high traffic,

98
00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:56.880
very high logging capacity requirements,

99
00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:59.670
then using KDF could be a very good thing.

100
00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:01.170
And then from Kinesis Data Firehose,

101
00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:04.680
you can send it to any destination supported by Kinesis,

102
00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:08.943
such as Amazon S3, Redshift, OpenSearch, and so on.

103
00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:13.320
So using WAF, there is a solution architecture, you know,

104
00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:16.080
which is to enhance CloudFront Origin Security.

105
00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:18.300
So let's say we have CloudFront in front

106
00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:21.870
of an application balancer talking to EC2 instances,

107
00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:23.010
and we want to make sure

108
00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:26.880
that only the traffic coming from Amazon CloudFront is

109
00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:29.220
allowed into the application balancer,

110
00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:31.170
and not, for example, site traffic.

111
00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:35.100
If we were to have the URL of the application balancer.

112
00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:36.390
So how would we do this?

113
00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:38.280
Well, using WAF, we can already set up

114
00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:41.400
some web ACLs on the CloudFront level.

115
00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:43.650
This is not going to help us with the application balancer,

116
00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:45.450
but at least we control some level

117
00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:48.180
of security on the clients.

118
00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:50.430
Okay, next, what's going to happen is that

119
00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:51.750
on Amazon CloudFront,

120
00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:55.530
you can create what's called a custom HTP header.

121
00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:59.310
And you have it for example, set to X Origin Verify

122
00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:01.380
and then a secret string.

123
00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:04.140
And so this will be added to every request that goes

124
00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:06.030
through Amazon CloudFront.

125
00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:08.880
The result of which is that we can also set up WAF

126
00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:11.170
on the application load balancer,

127
00:05:11.170 --> 00:05:14.700
and we create a filtering rule that says that any traffic

128
00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:18.990
that has this header, the X Origin verify

129
00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:20.670
and the value will be allowed

130
00:05:20.670 --> 00:05:23.370
and anything else will be rejected.

131
00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:24.600
So that means that someone,

132
00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:28.170
a user trying to access directly the ALB.

133
00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:30.810
Of course they don't know about the fact you're using custom

134
00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:33.060
HTP headers because they're hidden from the users,

135
00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:35.610
therefore they cannot access the ALB

136
00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:39.900
because they will be blocked by WAF directly on top of it.

137
00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:43.470
You can set up other rotation of this HTP header

138
00:05:43.470 --> 00:05:45.390
to have maximum security.

139
00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:48.000
So using Secrets Manager, we can have a secret

140
00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:51.300
that is automatically rotated using a Lambda function.

141
00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:54.450
And the Lambda function will update once in a while,

142
00:05:54.450 --> 00:05:58.920
the custom HTP header value with a new value in CloudFront,

143
00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:02.943
and of course update the filtering rule in AWS WAF.

144
00:06:04.290 --> 00:06:05.430
So that's it for this lecture.

145
00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:06.263
I hope you liked it,

146
00:06:06.263 --> 00:06:08.643
and I will see you in the next lecture.

